<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nemesis (1839)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nemesis was an iron-hulled paddle steamer built by John Laird at Birkenhead in 1839 for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, the first ocean-going iron warship. With a shallow draught and heavy guns, she served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a> from 1840, destroying Chinese war junks and leading British forces up rivers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, showing the power of steam warships against Qing defences.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nemesis-1839.jpg" alt="Nemesis (1839)" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: S. Bull (engraver) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HEIC_Nemesis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Nemesis was the largest of six similar vessels ordered by the Secret Committee of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>; her sister ships Phlegethon, Pluto, Proserpine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariadne">Ariadne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medusa">Medusa</a> came from John Laird's yard at Birkenhead and from William Fairbairn &amp; Sons at Millwall. Built in three months, she was 184 feet long, with a beam of 29 feet, a draught of 6 feet when full of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and a burthen of 660 tons. Driven by sail and by two sixty-horsepower Forrester engines, she carried two pivot-mounted 32-pounder and four 6-pounder guns.</p><p>Her watertight bulkheads, the first used in a warship, let her survive damage to her hull on her sea trials and on the voyage out. She did not appear in the Company's list of ships, and The Times suggested that she carried a letter of marque, adding that &quot;for the purpose of smuggling opium she is admirably adapted.&quot; To keep the voyage secret it was given out that she was bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a>. In July 1840 she became the first iron ship to round the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a> corrections devised by Sir George Airy, the Astronomer Royal, though her compass performed poorly throughout her career.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Commanded by Captain William Hutcheon Hall, she first saw action in the Second Battle of Chuenpi on 7 January 1841, against the Chinese fleet at the Bocca Tigris forts. At the Battle of First Bar on 27 February she sank the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, an old East Indiaman that the Chinese had bought and rearmed. Her shallow draught took her through shallow water in the Broadway expedition of 13 to 15 March, and she helped capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> on 18 March. The Chinese called her the &quot;devil ship&quot;. In 1842, under Captain Richard Collinson and based at Chusan, she fought at Taisam during the repulse of a much larger Chinese attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningbo">Ningbo</a>.</p><p>After the war she was sent against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, and then operated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, chiefly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>. At the end of 1846, after rioting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a>, Sir John Davis posted her to cover the Company's factory there, until she was withdrawn before February 1847. In May 1847 she attacked a flotilla of Sooloo pirate prows in the Brunei river, and early in 1853 she helped the Company's steamer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> drive Burmese troops out of Bassein province. She sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a> on 1 February 1854.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nemesis (1839 ship)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The First Opium War of 1839–1842 exposed the inadequacy of Qing forces against industrialised European militaries: a British fleet with modern artillery and steam-powered warships such as the iron steamer <b>Nemesis</b> overcame Qing coastal and Yangtze defences and forced the Treaty of Nanjing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
